## Policy (Po)

Run a whole rule-set against one subject and roll the answers up. Returns the roll-up status, per-status counts, coverage, and one line per rule in rule order.

```ts
import PolicyCommand from '@cardstack/catalog/cards/audit/commands/policy-command';

let result = await new PolicyCommand(commandContext).execute({
  subject,   // any card
  bot,       // an AuditorBot — or pass `rules` inline instead
} as any);
// result.rollup.status === 'partial'
// result.results → ['SUP-01 · pass · 94-2871234 · taxId holds a value.', …]
```

**The roll-up is deliberately pessimistic.** Any failure makes the subject non-compliant, and a clean run that still contains something unproven or unfireable is `partial`, never `pass`. A verdict must never be more confident than the parts it is made of — the single most common way a compliance dashboard lies.

`coverage` is conclusive verdicts over rules that applied, so a rule-set full of `unproven` reports low coverage rather than a clean bill.

Pure, like Condition: nothing is written. A report persists results; this only computes them, which is what lets a workspace preview a subject's standing without dirtying an audit.

## Example
A command is not a card instance. The natural target is `VendorProfile/acme-supply` with `AuditorBot/supplier-controls`: pass, pass, unproven, pass → **partial** at 75% coverage. `VendorProfile/norvik-ltd` gives pass, pass, fail, not-applicable → **fail**. Two subjects, one rule-set, every status the vocabulary has.

## Non-goals
Does not persist an Audit Result or raise Findings — Audit does both, and is the single writer. Does not select subjects; it judges the one it is handed.
